<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Aisin Gioro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aisin Gioro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Aisin Gioro is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> clan that ruled the Later Jin dynasty from 1616, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> from 1636 to 1912 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> from 1932 to 1945. Under the Ming its members were chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchens</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> created the clan as part of his reorganisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> society in 1601, naming the descendants of his father Taksi Aisin, 'gold'. It had no fixed rule of succession: an emperor named his heir in a secret edict. Princely titles fell by one rank with each generation, except for twelve 'iron-cap' peerages that passed on undiminished. The clan had 29,000 members when the Qing fell in 1912, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> kept his title in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> until 1924.</p></div>
